A||||10||carpetviper||Super or Turbo?||||5||
Z||000000||carpetviper||12-04-2001||03:08 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"> I know a lil' bit about both Super & turbo chargers, but don't know which really gives a better boost overall. By how they work, I would assume that a supercharger would give better results off the line than a turbo. Am I correct in this? I guess what I'm asking is what does each system offer over the other?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpetviper.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||88-DOHC||12-04-2001||03:55 PM||||Well, I am by no means an expert (nor claim to be) but I do know a little bit that might help you.  <P>Superchargers and turbos basicly do the same thing (giving you virtual displacement by forcing more air into the piston chamber). However each do this by making a compromise. They both have their own pro's and con's.  Superchargers give you added power right off, but steal power at higher RPM's. Turbos basicly don't give you anything at low RPM's but at higher RPM's pretty much give you added power for free. To make turbos trully effective, you usually use an intercooler. The exhaust spining the turbo tends to heat it up. Thus heating up the air on the intake side as well so you need to cool it before entering the engine. <P>Neither one is better then the other. They both do basicly the same thing in their own way. It just depends on what your looking for.  Want added low end torque, you would want a roots supercharger, want high end power, go turbo.<P>Thats basicly it in a nutshell.  One thing there are about 3 different types of superchargers, each different and each with their own pro's and con's<P>A URL that explains a little more about the differences.<BR><A HREF="http://freespace.virgin.net/c.coleman/supturb.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://freespace.virgin.net/c.coleman/supturb.htm</A><p>[This message has been edited by 88-DOHC (edited 12-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||sluppy123||12-04-2001||05:39 PM||||88-DOHC- "The exhaust spining the turbo tends to heat it up. Thus heating up the air on the intake side as well so you need to cool it before entering the engine." <P>Actually the air entering the intake is heated because it's being compressed, Same thing happens in a supercharger. If the proper size turbo and a well designed exhaust and intake are used, Turbo lag will be virtually eliminated<P>------------------<BR>-Brian, Toolmaker<BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>87' GT- Turbo Charged, Modified 2.8- 235 HP w/ 7 psi Boost,<BR>                 <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1711832&a=12781563&p=54316496&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Pyric||12-04-2001||06:44 PM||||It depends on what you want.  Most good superchargers don't steal enough HP to matter.  But Turbos cause resistance in the exhaust.  For a Fiero, which already has a problem with heat in the engine compartment, I'd back away from a turbo unless you have improved the engine's cooling capability.<P>Either way though, I've heard a stock V6 can't handle a lot of boost without breaking something.<P>Checkout <A HREF="http://www.eaton.com/supercharger/index.html#models" TARGET=_blank>http://www.eaton.com/supercharger/index.html#models</A> <P>I was considering a supercharger at one point.  These were the ones I was looking at.||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||12-04-2001||09:10 PM||||Most turbochargers will produce more boost than most superchargers that can be used on these engines (and fit). <P>At the same time, that extra boost will likely grenade most engines. <P>On the Fiero, the compromise isn't which blower to use, but what will fit in there. <P>You'll wind up with a turbo...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||carpetviper||12-05-2001||02:43 PM||||So a supercharger would be better to use for acceleration times off the line, but a turbo would be used for more power once moving? To use a supercharger must a sacrifice my A/C or is there a way around it?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><BR>BTW, thanks for the article, very good reading.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpetviper.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by carpetviper (edited 12-05-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000006||MinnGreenGT||12-05-2001||02:52 PM||||This is a lot of info at once, so read it thoroughly a few times to try and get it all... <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/014752.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/014752.html</A> <P>Triad & Turbo88 both have very strong points/opinions on which to use- read & enjoy!<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||carpetviper||12-05-2001||08:11 PM||||I read all that and I still don't know which is better.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"> However, my father brought up a good question that supports S/Cs. If S/Cs lack high end power, how come dragsters and such run S/Cs? You won't find many if any turbos on the strip on these beasts.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpetviper.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||carpetviper||12-06-2001||10:20 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000009||MinnGreenGT||12-06-2001||10:34 AM||||The idea behind that is that a S/C can rob an engine at the high end.  But when your engine is producing 2500hp, a 250-500hp loss is not as big a deal.  Whereas when you're only running 250hp, a 25-50hp loss can be much more dramatic.<P>I'm not saying I don't suggest or support a S/C... just answering your question.<P>Import drag racers (those 9 second ones...) utilize a progressive NOS system to kick the power up at the bottom end (before the turbo spools up).  That allows them to launch and get up to speed much quicker, since the turbo has lag.  (just the counter note to the original question).||||reg||1
Z||000010||turbo86se||12-06-2001||11:22 AM||||Personally, I don't know what's better. I've ridden (is that a word?)in Earl's 3.8SC'd coupe and it pulled like hell. My 86SE has a MillerWoods turbo on it, and it scares the he11 out of me sometimes, plenty of power when you need it. Can't wait to put in a 3.4<P>If you went to either Williamsburg show this year or last, you saw my Fiero. All black '86SE with grey leather and decklid scoop. I'd post a pic if I knew how.  <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/togo.gif"> <BR>Where are you in VA? If you ever make your way down to Myrtle Beach, LMK, I'll take you for a ride in it! <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/SC.jpg"> ||||reg||1
